#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
Compare charts/podiumd/etc/release-table.csv (see
export-confluence-release-table) against the CURRENT
charts/podiumd/Chart.yaml + values.yaml, and report:

  - version mismatches: a row's target_version_helm/target_version_app
    disagrees with what's actually pinned right now
  - missing from release-table.csv: a real Chart.yaml dependency with no
    row at all; a dependency that HAS row(s), but none of them has ever
    recorded a Helm chart version at all (see chart_version_ever_
    tracked — not merely "no change planned this cycle", which a blank
    target_version_helm on an otherwise-tracked row already means; see
    is_verifiable_target) — this catches a chart silently going
    untracked, e.g. because its own row lives on "Technische component
    versies", which has no Helm column at all to record one in; or an
    image pinned in values.yaml under a tracked component's own scope
    that no row's image_basename mentions — each finding's own second
    line names the Confluence "... component versies" table to add it
    to (see confluence_table_hint), plus exactly what the new row needs
    to say (see missing_image_hint/missing_chart_version_hint) for
    export-confluence-release-table's own component/image matching to
    resolve it right back to this same component/basename next time
  - missing from Chart.yaml/values.yaml: a row whose resolved "component"
    isn't a Chart.yaml dependency (or values.yaml top-level key, for a
    component with no separate Helm chart, e.g. frankgateway) anymore, or
    whose image_basename names an image no longer pinned there

A row's "component"/"alias"/"image_basename" columns are already resolved
by export-confluence-release-table — this reads them directly rather
than re-deriving anything from free-form text (component is always a
literal Chart.yaml dependency name, or a values.yaml top-level key for a
no-separate-chart component). The image lookup tries two lib.image.
version calls in the same ORDER update-image-version/verify-
image-version/show-image-baseline-version's own <key> <basename>
resolution does (lib.image.version.resolve_scoped_matches): first
basenames_under_scope_any_tag, scoped to the component's own values.yaml
subtree (fast, and the common case); if that misses, a plain find_
matches_any_tag search across the WHOLE file, since a basename is a real
repository identity, not a values.yaml path, and can legitimately be
pinned under a sibling scope instead (e.g. keycloak-config-cli lives
under top-level "keycloak", not its own component's "keycloak-operator")
— this script reports that as a finding either way, rather than
requiring an exact <key> match the way those other tools do. Deliberately
the "_any_tag" siblings, not the plain (digest-required)
basenames_under_scope/find_matches those other tools themselves use:
release-table.csv only ever records a version string, never a digest, so
whether an image happens to be digest-pinned is irrelevant to every
comparison THIS script makes — on either the current-values.yaml side or
the release_table-baseline side (see check_images_source below). A chart
old enough to predate this repo's own digest-pinning convention (real
case: podiumd-4.8.5, this chart's own release_table baseline as of this
writing) still compares correctly on version alone. The chart-version
lookup is a plain Chart.yaml dependency read, the same one update-
component-version's own pre-write gate uses — never touches either tag
scanner at all, digest-required or not.

A row whose component is "MULTIPLE" is a shared base image hoisted into
values.yaml's top-level global.images map (see export-confluence-release-
table.py's own global_image_keys/component_and_alias — e.g. nginx, curl,
busybox, pinned once and reused via YAML anchor across several unrelated
components) rather than owned by any single component — its image_basename
is still resolved by export time, though, so it's checked here the exact
same way as any other component's images, just scoped to "global" instead
of a Chart.yaml dependency's own values.yaml block (and with no chart-
version check, since it isn't backed by a separate Helm chart at all). A
"MULTIPLE" row export couldn't even resolve an image_basename for (an
ambiguous plain dependency-name collision, not a global image) has nothing
to check either way and is silently skipped, same as blank.

A row whose component is "UNKNOWN" (export-confluence-release-table
couldn't resolve it to anything at export time) is listed separately as
unresolved — that's a pre-existing export-time gap, not something this
script can verify either way, so it isn't scored as a failure here.

Purely local and network-free (no `helm pull`, no registry calls): this
compares release-table.csv's OWN image_basename column against whatever
is ALREADY pinned in values.yaml right now, so nothing needs pulling.
Even a "fallback path" component's own primary image (one with no
explicit "repository:" override in podiumd's own values.yaml at all,
relying entirely on its subchart's own default — e.g. openzaak,
opennotificaties) resolves its basename locally, from whatever .tgz is
ALREADY vendored under charts/podiumd/charts/ (see lib.chart.
primary_image_repositories) — never a fresh pull. If that subchart isn't
vendored at its currently-pinned version, the primary basename for that
component just can't be resolved yet (see primary_image_basename) — the
same "not pulling" tradeoff verify-component-version makes explicitly,
just silent here.

Also compares version text literally, not semantically: a "v" prefix
difference between the two sides (e.g. release-table "1.89.0" vs
values.yaml "v1.89.0") is reported as a mismatch even though the actual
version is the same — the release-table column and the values.yaml pin
are supposed to record the identical string, so a drift there is itself
worth surfacing rather than silently normalized away.

--baseline-only skips every target-side check (check_chart_version/
check_images — no target_version_app/target_version_helm comparison at
all) and runs only the source-side ones (check_chart_version_source/
check_images_source), against the release_table baseline. It ALSO widens
those two source-side checks themselves: normally, a row with a blank
source_version_app/source_version_helm is silently skipped entirely — "no
value recorded yet" is assumed to mean "this item didn't exist yet at the
release_table baseline", never verified. Under --baseline-only, that
assumption itself is checked: a blank source is only left silent if the
item genuinely can't be found (via every tier the non-blank comparison
already uses) at the release_table baseline; if it turns out the item
DID already exist there, that's a real finding (a "...
-PRESENCE" tag) — the CSV should have recorded a source version for it
but didn't. Default (no flag) behavior — both the target-side checks and
the blank-source short-circuit — is completely unaffected.

If the release_table baseline itself can't be resolved at all (see the
WARNING this prints either way), --baseline-only has nothing left to
check — the CLI errors out (exit 1) rather than silently reporting "OK".

Usage:
    verify-release-table-with-podiumd
    verify-release-table-with-podiumd --baseline-only

Exit code is non-zero if any version mismatch, missing-from-release-table,
or missing-from-chart-or-values item was found.
"""

import csv
import sys

from pathlib import Path

SCRIPT_DIR = Path(__file__).resolve().parent
sys.path.insert(0, str(SCRIPT_DIR))

# Not installed as a package -- sys.path must be patched before
# lib.* can be imported, so these imports can't move to the top.
# pylint: disable=wrong-import-position
from lib.chart.release_baseline_basics import load_yaml
from lib.chart.release_baseline_basics import release_table_baseline
from lib.dependencies import require_vendored_dependencies
from lib.release_baseline import resolve_baseline_chart_state
from lib.release_table_verification import ChartState
from lib.release_table_verification import compare

# isort: off
# Fenced from import sorting: isort and ruff's isort rules attach these
# comment lines to different imports.
#
# basenames_under_scope_any_tag isn't used by this script's own code
# below -- kept imported only so the tests/ suite's `vrt` fixture (which
# loads this whole script as a module) can still reach it as
# vrt.basenames_under_scope_any_tag.
# pylint: disable-next=unused-import
from lib.image.version import basenames_under_scope_any_tag  # noqa: F401

# primary_image_repositories: same test-only re-export reason as
# basenames_under_scope_any_tag above (vrt.primary_image_repositories,
# monkeypatched by tests to fake subchart-fallback image resolution).
# pylint: disable-next=unused-import
from lib.chart.pull_and_subchart_resolution import primary_image_repositories  # noqa: F401

# is_primary_image: same test-only re-export reason as
# basenames_under_scope_any_tag above (vrt.is_primary_image).
# pylint: disable-next=unused-import
from lib.release_table_verification import is_primary_image  # noqa: F401
# isort: on

CHART_DIR = SCRIPT_DIR.parents[0]
CHART_YAML = CHART_DIR / "Chart.yaml"
VALUES_YAML = CHART_DIR / "values.yaml"
RELEASE_TABLE_CSV = CHART_DIR / "etc" / "release-table.csv"


def load_release_table(path):
    """The release-table.csv at `path`, parsed as a list of dict rows
    (csv.DictReader) — one row per (component, section) pair the way
    export-confluence-release-table writes it; see its own CSV_HEADER for
    the column set."""
    with path.open(newline="", encoding="utf-8") as f:
        return list(csv.DictReader(f))


SECTIONS = [
    ("mismatches", "Version mismatches"),
    ("ambiguous", "Ambiguous pins (can't verify)"),
    ("missing_from_release_table", "Missing from release-table.csv"),
    ("missing_from_chart", "Missing from Chart.yaml / values.yaml"),
]


def print_report(findings, unresolved):
    """Prints every non-empty findings[key] section (in SECTIONS' order,
    items sorted), plus a "Skipped" block for `unresolved` rows (see
    compare — a release-table.csv row whose component couldn't be
    resolved to a Chart.yaml dependency or values.yaml key at all), or a
    single "OK" line when there's nothing to report. Returns True if
    anything was printed, so main() can use it directly as the process's
    exit-status signal."""
    any_findings = False
    for key, title in SECTIONS:
        items = findings.get(key)
        if not items:
            continue
        any_findings = True
        print(f"\n{title}:")
        for item in sorted(items):
            print(f"  {item}")

    if unresolved:
        print(f"\nSkipped ({len(unresolved)} row(s) with an unresolved component in release-table.csv):")
        for row in sorted(unresolved, key=lambda r: r["name"]):
            print(f"  - '{row['name']}' (component={row['component'] or '(blank)'})")

    if not any_findings:
        print("OK: release-table.csv matches Chart.yaml / values.yaml")
    return any_findings


def _resolve_baseline_state(chart_dir):
    """The release_table baseline's own ChartState (see main()), or None
    with a WARNING already printed — either because release-baseline.yaml
    has no release_table key set yet, or that ref couldn't be resolved
    (see lib.release_baseline.resolve_baseline_chart_state). Either way,
    None is exactly what tells compare() to skip every source-side check
    entirely rather than risk a false "didn't exist at baseline" flood
    (see compare()'s own docstring)."""
    baseline_ref = release_table_baseline(chart_dir)
    if baseline_ref is None:
        print("WARNING: release_table baseline not set in release-baseline.yaml — skipping source-version checks\n")
        return None
    _resolved_ref, deps, values, lines, error = resolve_baseline_chart_state(chart_dir, baseline_ref)
    if error:
        print(f'WARNING: release_table baseline "{baseline_ref}": {error} — skipping source-version checks\n')
        return None
    return ChartState(chart_dir, deps, values, lines)


def main():
    """CLI entry point: loads release-table.csv plus Chart.yaml/values.yaml
    (and, when release-baseline.yaml's release_table key resolves, the
    same state as of that baseline — see resolve_baseline_chart_state),
    runs compare() over every dependency/component, prints the results
    via print_report, and exits 1 if anything was found. `--baseline-only`
    restricts the run to the source-side presence checks (see compare's
    own docstring) and requires the baseline to have resolved; with no
    resolvable baseline otherwise, the source-version checks are skipped
    with a warning rather than failing the whole run."""
    args = sys.argv[1:]
    if args in (["-h"], ["--help"]):
        print(__doc__)
        sys.exit(0)
    if args not in ([], ["--baseline-only"]):
        print(__doc__)
        sys.exit(1)
    baseline_only = args == ["--baseline-only"]
    if not baseline_only:
        # --baseline-only skips every target-side check, the only ones
        # that read this checkout's own vendored charts/*.tgz.
        require_vendored_dependencies(CHART_DIR)

    if not RELEASE_TABLE_CSV.is_file():
        print(f"error: {RELEASE_TABLE_CSV} not found")
        print("Create it first with export-confluence-release-table.")
        sys.exit(1)
    rows = load_release_table(RELEASE_TABLE_CSV)

    chart_yaml = load_yaml(CHART_YAML) if CHART_YAML.is_file() else {}
    deps = (chart_yaml or {}).get("dependencies", [])
    values = load_yaml(VALUES_YAML) if VALUES_YAML.is_file() else {}
    lines = VALUES_YAML.read_text(encoding="utf-8").splitlines() if VALUES_YAML.is_file() else []

    # None on ANY failure here — see compare()'s own docstring for why
    # that distinction matters: a baseline that couldn't be resolved
    # must skip the new source-checks entirely, never be silently
    # treated as "resolved, genuinely empty", which would flood every
    # row with a false finding instead. The existing target-checks are
    # completely unaffected either way — only the new source-checks are
    # ever gated on this.
    baseline = _resolve_baseline_state(CHART_DIR)

    if baseline_only and baseline is None:
        print(
            "error: --baseline-only has nothing to check — the release_table baseline could not be "
            "resolved (see the WARNING above)"
        )
        sys.exit(1)

    findings, unresolved = compare(
        rows,
        ChartState(CHART_DIR, deps, values, lines),
        baseline=baseline,
        baseline_only=baseline_only,
    )
    any_findings = print_report(findings, unresolved)
    sys.exit(1 if any_findings else 0)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
